WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.335
Detection engineers rarely work alone.

2
00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:15.465
Their work sits between telemetry owners, security operations, threat intelligence, threat hunting, incident response, purple teaming, architecture, DevSecOps, and business risk owners.

3
00:00:15.465 --> 00:00:21.981
SOC analysts provide essential feedback because they see how alerts behave during real investigations.

4
00:00:21.981 --> 00:00:28.806
They can explain which fields are helpful, which alerts create repetitive noise, and which response paths are unclear.

5
00:00:28.806 --> 00:00:32.697
Threat hunters and incident responders contribute different perspectives.

6
00:00:32.697 --> 00:00:42.066
Hunters may identify behaviors that deserve durable detection, while responders may identify visibility gaps discovered during an investigation.

7
00:00:42.066 --> 00:00:47.624
Threat intelligence helps detection teams prioritize behaviors that matter to the organization.

8
00:00:47.624 --> 00:00:55.646
Purple team exercises and controlled validation help teams test whether detections behave as intended under realistic conditions.

9
00:00:55.646 --> 00:01:00.924
This collaboration is what makes detection engineering operational instead of theoretical.

10
00:01:00.924 --> 00:01:08.876
Detections should support the people who use them, fit the environment where they run, and improve as teams learn from day-to-day security work.

11
00:01:08.876 --> 00:01:12.678
Collaboration also prevents blind spots caused by assumptions.

12
00:01:12.678 --> 00:01:24.721
A detection engineer may understand query logic, while a platform owner understands logging behavior, an analyst understands triage pain, and a responder understands incident impact.

13
00:01:24.721 --> 00:01:30.219
When those perspectives come together, the result is usually better than a rule built in isolation.

14
00:01:30.219 --> 00:01:37.033
The detection is more likely to have the right data, useful context, realistic thresholds, and clear next steps.

15
00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:39.758
Regular feedback channels make this sustainable.

16
00:01:39.758 --> 00:01:48.707
Review meetings, detection tickets, analyst comments, post-incident actions, and validation results can all become inputs to the detection backlog.
